Yeah, another problem with the ISO ones is that they suffer from the same=
=20
problem that many philosophical languages (where words are built from a=20
classification system rather than arbitrarily) have, namely, poor error=20
detection/redundancy, especially when spoken. All those little phonemes at=
=20
the end of those words are carrying a lot of disambiguating information;=20
mishearing one of them could have you thinking of an entirely different=20
country, and since it will be used in sentences where you expect to hear a=
=20
country in, context isn't often much help.

